Use the runtime's daylight saving bias for the %z offset in strftime().
The %z emulation took daylight saving time to be one hour ahead of
standard time, but the C runtime's localtime() uses the bias in
_dstbias, which comes from the Windows time zone. In a zone whose bias
is not an hour, such as Lord Howe Island's 30 minutes, %z gave an offset
that didn't match the local time printed beside it, as in date -R,
date -Iseconds, and ls --full-time.

Add _dstbias, which is negative, instead of subtracting 3600 seconds.

---
 win32/mingw.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/mingw.c
+++ b/win32/mingw.c
@@ -2228,7 +2228,7 @@
 			else if ( t[1] == 'z' ) {
 				_tzset();
 				if ( tm->tm_isdst >= 0 ) {
-					int offset = (int)_timezone - (tm->tm_isdst > 0 ? 3600 : 0);
+					int offset = (int)_timezone + (tm->tm_isdst > 0 ? (int)_dstbias : 0);
 					int hr, min;
 
 					if ( offset > 0 ) {
